    "canonical_url": "https://www.powermag.com/stop-viewing-battery-passports-as-compliance-theyre-the-missing-link-to-monetize-second-life-batteries/",
    "origin_publisher": "POWER Magazine",
    "published_at": "2026-10-22T12:36:58+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-22T12:36:58+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-22T14:59:07.993415+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-22T14:59:07.993415+00:00",
    "body": "What does the upcoming European Union (EU) Battery Passport look like to many energy executives? Another regulatory requirement. Another documentation demand. Another checkbox.\nIn other words, another headache.\nBut viewing a battery passport as merely a component of compliance infrastructure overlooks its potential as a key piece of financial infrastructure.\nWhy? Because second-life batteries have an image problem. Despite their possible remaining value, buyers, lenders, insurers, and developers don’t have enough trusted information to confidently determine what that value is. Buying a used battery without that history is like buying a used vehicle without a Carfax report.\nStandardized battery data could make these previously opaque assets easier to value, finance, insure, and resell. In that sense, a battery passport could become the Carfax report for batteries. It may not make the battery more valuable by itself, but it certainly makes the battery’s existing value more measurable and trustworthy.\nAnd that makes a battery passport more of an opportunity than an obligation.\nThe Biggest Discount on Second-Life Batteries Is Uncertainty\nEvery time someone asks me how much an electric vehicle (EV) battery’s performance changes with temperature, I’m reminded how little most people know about a battery’s history. Now imagine trying to underwrite one as a multimillion-dollar asset.\nFinancial institutions have traditionally cast a skeptical eye on second-life batteries, mostly because they don’t have all the information they need about them. For example, without a verifiable, standardized rating, it’s impossible to answer key valuation questions like:\nHow much useful life remains?\nHow has the battery been treated?\nWhat operating conditions has it experienced?\nHow quickly has it degraded?\nWhat performance can a new owner realistically expect?\nIn other words, a second-life battery is a bundle of unknowns. And when lenders cannot confidently forecast future performance, they have to build uncertainty into their assessment of the asset.\nContrast this situation with the used car market and the availability of Carfax information, which creates a trusted record that reduces information asymmetry.\nA Battery Passport Can Turn Battery History into an Underwriting Dataset\nThe power of a battery passport doesn’t lie in any single metric. Rather, it’s the combination of standardized data points that creates a comprehensive picture of an asset’s remaining economic life. For example, a financially useful battery history might contain things like:\nCharge/discharge cycle count.\nRate of degradation over time.\nTemperature exposure, including extreme heat and cold.\nTime to charge and capacity.\nOverall state of health.\nBroader performance history and provenance.\nThe real value comes from bringing these inputs together in one place. With better historical data, institutions can create better forecasts. With better forecasts, they can have more confidence in expected future performance. And with more confidence, they can make better valuation, insurance, and financing decisions.\nWhy? Because financiers aren’t simply buying “a battery.” They’re investing in that battery’s expected future performance and cash-generating potential.\nI’ve seen the difference that information can make in the real world. My team has helped original equipment manufacturers (OEMs) aggregate battery data, ingest and clean telematics, and package that information into passports. And that resulting information has helped dealers market vehicles more effectively, while giving customers greater confidence in what they’re buying.\nThat’s not just another regulation. Having trusted battery information changes the whole transaction. And if better data can change the economics of a used EV transaction, think of the possibilities as the battery moves into its second life as an energy-storage asset.\nMaking the Move from Compliance to Opportunity\nThis is where the conversation around battery passports moves beyond compliance. Compliance may get you into the game, but the competitive advantage comes from monetizing the data and delivering value to customers.\nA verified battery history, for example, could support a residual-value model that updates as the battery ages. That model could then inform resale pricing, insurance underwriting, warranty terms, or financing.\nLet’s go back to the Carfax business model. Here, a dealer or other intermediary could use a trusted passport or certification to demonstrate that a battery’s key data has been verified. The consumer could receive that information as part of the transaction, while the dealer pays for the service because the certification has commercial value.\nThe value prospects go beyond the automotive industry. Utilities and energy developers, for example, could use second-life batteries for storage and microgrids. What they need is the confidence that enough useful life remains to justify the investment. Battery passports can help reduce uncertainty and improve capital efficiency.\nThat doesn’t mean the EU framework will automatically become U.S. law. But global manufacturers have strong incentives to avoid building entirely separate data infrastructures for different markets, making the EU standard strategically relevant to U.S. companies as well.\nAnd companies that build the infrastructure to meet that standard early (and capitalize on it) can establish a stronger market position before the ecosystem matures.\nThe Battery Passport Is the Foundation for the Second-Life Battery Market\nSecond-life batteries are full of untapped potential, but the market for them lacks the confidence in how to measure that value.\nCarfax’s ability to standardize history transformed the used-car market by making uncertainty more manageable. Battery passports could perform a similar function for second-life energy storage.\nOnce the market can make battery health transparent, trusted, and standardized, second-life batteries can move from “scary unknown” to an asset class investors actively want to finance.\nThat’s more than a regulatory checkbox. That’s data as an asset, with all the potential upside that goes along with it.\n— Michelle Blakeley serves as vice president, Auto & Industrials at Perficient .\nTagged in:\nBattery Data\nBattery Health\nBattery Passport\nEU Regulation\nResidual Value\nsecond-life batteries\nUnderwriting\nSept. 28—30, 2026\nWashington, D.C.\nRegister Now\nExplore this topic and more — live at Experience POWER. The power industry's most urgent decisions are being made right now. Be in the room where they happen.\nView Conference Program",
